# Fallow CLI (Windows / PowerShell)

Run fallow commands plainly. No shell workarounds.

## NEVER do this

* ❌ `$env:FALLOW_AGENT_SOURCE=...` or any other PowerShell env-var assignment for fallow
* ❌ `$LASTEXITCODE`, `exit 0`, `if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 1)`, or other exit-code manipulation after fallow
* ❌ Bash-only patterns on Windows: `2>/dev/null`, `2>$null`, `|| true`, `; if ($LASTEXITCODE ...)`

Exit code **1** from fallow means **issues found** — that is a normal result, not a command failure. Do not try to mask it.

## Fix findings properly

When fallow reports dead code, duplication, or complexity: **fix the underlying problem**. Do not silence the signal.

## NEVER do this (findings)

* ❌ `// fallow-ignore-next-line`, `// fallow-ignore-file`, or any other `fallow-ignore` suppression to hide an issue
* ❌ Prefixing identifiers with `_` (e.g. `_unused`, `_variable`) or similar hacks to dodge unused-export/unused-variable findings
* ❌ Leaving duplicated code in place and annotating it instead of extracting a shared helper or consolidating logic

## NEVER do this (thresholds / config)

* ❌ Editing `.fallowrc.json` (or any other fallow config) to relax thresholds, raise limits, turn rules off, or add overrides — just to make checks pass
* ❌ Proposing or applying config changes (complexity caps, duplication thresholds, `ignorePatterns`, `health.ignore`, per-file rule overrides, etc.) without the user explicitly asking for that
* Threshold and policy changes are **project decisions for the user**, not a workaround when fallow reports issues. Fix the code instead; if a threshold truly needs changing, explain why and let the user decide.

## Always do this instead (findings)

* Remove truly unused exports, files, and dependencies
* Extract shared helpers for duplicated code (e.g. shared modules, hooks, utilities)
* Refactor complex functions when complexity is the issue
* If an export is intentionally public API, justify it structurally (e.g. re-export from a barrel, document as public entry) — not via ignore comments

## Always do this instead (CLI)

* Run from project root with **`bunx fallow <command>`** (or `bun run check:fallow` for the full gate)
* Use JSON output when parsing results: `bunx fallow dead-code --format json --quiet`
* Before `fix`, always dry-run first: `bunx fallow fix --dry-run --format json --quiet`
* Apply fixes only after review: `bunx fallow fix --yes --format json --quiet`

